This is a proof of concept Wayland WSEGL, which enables the use of SGX
hardware buffers as pixmaps, effectively making wayland available on SGX
devices.

To use it, you want to do something like this:
- mv /usr/lib/libEGL.so /usr/lib/libEGL-sgx.so
- install the libEGl "proxy" (it'll send calls to libEGL-sgx.so, and do some extra
  lifting for wayland)
- install the wsegl plugin
- edit /etc/powervr.ini, adding the following to the [default] section:
  WindowSystem=/usr/lib/waylandwsegl.so

Make sure you have X and anything using X killed off.

To test it, you'll want to run e.g. qml-compositor with something like this:
    cd /usr/lib/qt5/examples/qtwayland/qml-compositor/
    QT_QPA_EGLFS_DEPTH=16 QT_COMPOSITOR_NEGATE_INVERTED_Y=1 ./qml-compositor -platform eglfs

And you can then run clients like this:
    QT_QPA_EGLFS_DEPTH=16 QT_WAYLAND_DISABLE_WINDOWDECORATION=1 /usr/lib/qt5/bin/qmlscene -platform wayland test.qml

Debugging:
WAYLAND_DEBUG=1
WSEGL_DEBUG=5
LIBEGL=/usr/lib/libEGL-sgx.so
... other magical incantations?

Rendering synchronization:
- Each EGL window owns its frame callback and event queue. Render and destroy
  each window on its owning render thread; destroy its EGLSurface before calling
  wl_egl_window_destroy(). Separate windows may use separate render threads.
- Back buffers become writable again only after wl_buffer.release. Frame
  callbacks pace rendering but do not transfer buffer ownership.
- Drawable deletion detaches the surface and drains outstanding buffer releases
  before freeing exported storage. Resize uses this same path.
- eglSwapInterval(0) disables frame-callback throttling, while still waiting for
  buffer release when both buffers are busy.

Rebuild and install libwayland-egl and waylandwsegl together: they share the
private window structure. Target builds still require the legacy Wayland server
headers and TI SGX/PVR2D development files used by this project.

Host regression tests:
    sh tests/run.sh
    sh tests/run.sh --sanitize

The tests need C/C++ compilers, pkg-config, wayland-scanner, modern Wayland client
and server development libraries, and timeout. They exercise the production
rendering code with simulated PVR allocations and ordered Wayland events in both
debug and NDEBUG builds, plus a real Wayland socket test with concurrent event
queues. The sanitizer option enables address, undefined-behavior and leak checks.
They do not validate SGX execution or OMAP framebuffer ioctls on real hardware.
